
  This is a scope decision, not a detail. Notifications are a reaction to task changes, so the task model defines the
  entire event vocabulary. Whatever fields a task has are the only things that can ever change, and therefore the only
  things anyone can be notified about. Picking too thin a model quietly caps the feature; picking too fat a one turns
  this into "build a task manager" with notifications as a footnote.

  - [1mMinimal but real[0m — tasks get [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mtitle[39m, [38;5;153mstatus[39m, [38;5;153massignee[39m, [38;5;153mupdatedAt[39m, plus create/edit/delete UI. Enough to generate
    genuine change events and demo the whole notification loop end to end. Costs: you're accepting a task model now, and
    adding fields later means adding event types later. Cheap to extend, since each new field is additive.
  - [1mAssumed task interface, no tasks UI[0m — define the task shape as a contract, build only the notifications layer
    against it. Buys a tightly-scoped spec. Costs: nothing is demonstrable or testable against real user actions, and
    the contract is unvalidated guesswork. This is the option I'd push back on.
  - [1mRicher task app[0m — projects/tags, due dates, comments, priority. Buys a much more interesting notification feature
    (subscribe to a project, get notified on comments). Costs: the task app becomes the bulk of the work, and this stops
    being one spec — it'd need decomposing into at least two.

  [1mMy recommendation: minimal but real.[0m It's the smallest thing that makes notifications genuinely testable, and every
  field you add later is additive rather than a rework. One caveat I want to name now regardless of your answer: in a
  client-only app there's effectively one person, so every change is [3myour own[0m — and notifying you about your own edits
  is noise. We'll need a deliberate answer for that (seeded demo actors, an "acting as" switcher, or
  suppress-self-changes), and I'll bring it as the next question.
